raw:
{
  "slots": {
    "question": [
      {
        "claim": "Does a Democratic campaign ad that labels Trump a “convicted criminal” change how Democrats judge people with criminal records generally, compared with answering before seeing the ad?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Being presented with the Democratic party’s Trump advertisement alone, labeling him as a “convicted\ncriminal,” will significantly decrease (positive) perceptions of individuals with criminal records, on average, relative\nto not being exposed to this advertisement",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Does the ad change how well Democrats feel represented by their party or how they judge elected officials’ motives?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "to what extent does the propagation of such stigmatizing terminology impact how well\nDemocrats perceive to be represented by their own elected officials?",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "their perceptions of elected officials’ motivations\n(i.e., the extent to which they are motivated by serving the public, as opposed to their own self-interest.)",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The primary question is whether hearing a formerly incarcerated representative’s perspective before seeing the ad changes the response to it, including support for its use, judgments of people with records, and views of representation.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the\nprimary purpose of this study was to test whether providing a counter-narrative prior to being presented with the Trump\nadvertisement significantly impacted how Democrats responded to the advertisement.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "Exposure to the counter-narrative first, describing a public service-oriented individual who has a criminal record, will significantly decrease support for the use of the advertisement, on average, relative to exposure to\nthe advertisement of Trump being labeled as a “convicted criminal” alone.",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "A secondary question is whether the treatments affect respondents differently if they or someone close to them have been involved in the justice system.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "supplemental analyses will also explore potential heterogeneity by\nsystem-impact status (i.e., whether someone identifies as having previously been involved in the system or impacted by\nthe system through their loved one’s involvement.)",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "central_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "The ad produced a trade-off: Democrats felt better represented, but the harm to their judgments of people with records was considerably larger than the gain in ratings of representation. Hearing a formerly incarcerated lawmaker first nearly eliminated that harm.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the magnitude of these effects (approximately 0.30 to 0.34 in absolute value SD units) are considerably smaller than\nthe advertisement’s effects on perceptions of individuals with criminal records (approximately 0.83)",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "but when paired with (and preceded by) a counter-narrative of a dedicated,\nsystem-impacted state representative who speaks to the harm of the “criminal” label, these effects were almost entirely\nameliorated ( β̂2 = 0.833, whereas β̂0 = −0.835).",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Harney argues for testing a different campaign choice: acknowledge Trump’s convictions and his response to them while giving formerly incarcerated representatives a voice, rather than treating a criminal record itself as grounds for condemnation.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "a viable, alternate route for political messaging\nduring the 2024 presidential election could have been to acknowledge, but juxtapose, the nature of Trump’s convictions and his\nbroadcasted attitude about his convictions with effective counter-narratives.",
            "page": 18
          },
          {
            "text": "utilizing political messaging\nthat elevates dedicated, justice-involved voices within the party rather than rely on\nstigmatizing attacks should be strongly considered and tested.",
            "page": 1
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "evidence": [
      {
        "claim": "The study recruited 2,500 fluent English-speaking U.S. Democrats through Prolific on July 12–13, 2025. The final sample numbered 2,439; 21.4% identified as personally or indirectly affected by the justice system.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "2,500 participants were\nrecruited for this study, between July 12th and 13th of 2025 [60]. This survey was fielded amongst individuals residing within\nthe United States who spoke fluent English and were members of the Democratic Party.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "No observations were flagged as coming from the\nsame IP addressing, leaving the remaining, final analytical sample size of 2,439 participants.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "21.4% of the sample identified as being system-impacted.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Data screening removed failed attention checks, duplicate IDs, incorrect or missing completion codes, and responses flagged as unlikely to be human.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "2,481 were retained after dropping those who failed the attention check,\nwere duplicate observations based on stored Prolific identifier embedded data, or provided the wrong completion code",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "Approximately 1.69% of responses post-removal of duplicates, incorrect/no codes, and failed attention checks were\nflagged as not being a likely human response, and were also dropped.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Random assignment varied the order of exposure. The control group answered the main outcomes first; the ad-alone group saw the ad before those outcomes; the narrative-first group read the lawmaker’s account, saw the ad, then answered. Ad support was always measured immediately after the ad.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the 2,439 participants were allocated approximately evenly across one of three different treatment groups: a) the\nControl group (n = 813); b) the Trump Ad Alone group (n = 814); and c) the Counter-Narrative First group (n = 812).",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "the Control group, which presented all outcome questions first, followed by the Trump advertisement stimulus (and associated questions related to the advertisement), followed by the counter-narrative.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "The Trump Ad Alone group\npresented participants with the advertisement (and its associated questions) first, followed by the remaining outcome\nquestions, and subsequently the counter-narrative. Finally, the Counter-Narrative First group presented the Trump advertisement (and its associated questions) only after being shown the counter-narrative. Then the outcomes were presented.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "the advertisement support question, which is asked immediately after being presented with the advertisement itself.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The ad stimulus was an actual 2024 campaign image: Trump’s mugshot with a capitalized criminal label. The image and its description did not vary across groups.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Regardless of when participants were presented with the Trump advertisement, the content was (importantly) constant\nacross treatment arms.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "one of the images\nutilized in advertisements within the 2024 presidential election, specifically President Donald J. Trump’s mugshot, which\nwas labeled, in all capital letters, “CONVICTED CRIMINAL.”",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The counter-narrative was a lightly edited Nevada Current excerpt, not another image. It described Jovan Jackson’s reform advocacy, why the campaign’s contrast between prosecutor and felon hurt him, and the distinction he drew between rehabilitation and Trump’s response.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "This excerpt was from the article entitled, “Could a formerly incarcerated lawmaker reshape justice policy in the NV\nLegislature?,” by Michael Lyle, published in the Nevada Current on November 5, 2024 [55].",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "Every election cycle he knocked on doors for candidates who backed criminal justice reforms, and every legislative session he would lobby for what he believes are smarter policies that affect\npeople impacted by the justice system.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "Jackson found the contrast hurtful and undercut the work he had done since being incarcerated. He said there is a clear difference between a formerly incarcerated person who sought rehabilitation, served\ntheir time and was released, and Trump.",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Four outcomes were averaged, standardized indices: attitudes toward people with records, quality of federal Democratic representation, officials’ public-service motives, and support for the ad. The ad index distinguished perceived effectiveness from explicit endorsement.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The remaining four outcomes are measured as indices, averaged over each question included in the index, and subsequently standardized for ease of interpretation.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "The primary outcomes of interest in this study were: a) perceptions of individuals with criminal records; b) perceived value\nof representation; c) perceived quality of Democratic representation in federal government; d) complexity of representation; e) motives of elected officials; and f) advertisement support.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "a) “This is an effective advertisement;” and b) “I support use of this advertisement.”",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Two single-item measures separated valuing representatives’ diverse life experiences from believing that such diversity benefits constituents only when accompanied by effective advocacy.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "“Having representatives with a wide array of life experiences is crucial to strengthening our democracy,”",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "“Having representatives\nwith a wide array of life experiences is really only beneficial when our representatives effectively advocate for the needs of\ntheir constituents.”",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The analysis estimated preregistered between-group contrasts by least squares with HC-2 robust standard errors, reporting both unadjusted estimates and estimates adjusted for demographics, justice-system impact, age, and prior racial attitudes. Balance checks found no significant differences for the pairs compared.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "To estimate each ATE, least squares regressions with heteroskedasticity-consistent standard error\nestimators, HC-2, were utilized. Both the treatment indicator only and the primary models (which include covariates, as\npre-specified), are reported for transparency.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "a) gender; b) race and/or ethnicity; c) education (less than four year degree indicator); d) whether\nsomeone identifies as being system-impacted or not; e) age; and f) the pre-treatment racial attitudes index.",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "there is no statistical evidence of imbalance between treatment arms that are being compared to one another\nin this study.",
            "page": 9
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "A manipulation check measured emotional response to the narrative. Separate secondary analyses examined individual attitude items, background predictors, and variation by justice-system impact.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "a manipulation check was included after exposure to the\nnarrative, specifically asking participants to state the extent to which they agreed with the statement, “I had an emotional\nreaction to this narrative.”",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "on individual items for the perceptions of individuals with criminal records index, given its marginally acceptable reliability\ncoefficient; b) summarization of the sociodemographic factors that were significantly predictive of the outcomes of interest;\nand c) heterogeneity in results by system-impact status.",
            "page": 12
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "key_findings": [
      {
        "claim": "Seeing the ad before answering lowered favorable attitudes toward people with records by 0.835 standard deviations relative to control. Reading Jackson’s account first raised those attitudes by 0.833 standard deviations relative to the ad alone: the two contrasts were nearly equal and opposite.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "exposure to the Trump advertisement alone significantly decreased positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records ( β̂0 = −0.835, SE = 0.044)",
            "page": 11
          },
          {
            "text": "these effects were almost entirely\nameliorated ( β̂2 = 0.833, whereas β̂0 = −0.835).",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The ad improved ratings of Democratic representation; hearing Jackson first lowered those ratings relative to the ad alone. Belief in officials’ public-service motives followed the same pattern, but the motives findings did not survive correction for multiple comparisons.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "providing the counter-narrative also worsened perceptions of the quality of Democratic\nrepresentation, relative to the Trump advertisement alone. Additionally, exposure to the Trump advertisement significantly increased feelings of being well represented by elected officials and the extent to which Democrats perceived\nthat elected officials were motivated by public service over self-interest.",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "reduced the extent to which participants believed that elected officials’ motivations were to serve\nthe public, versus serving their own self-interest ( β̂2 = −0.098, SE = 0.048).",
            "page": 11
          },
          {
            "text": "Of the above significant outcomes, only the\npublic-serving motives outcome would not be significant after multiple comparisons corrections, particularly Bonferroni.",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "only the public-serving motives outcome would not be significant after multiple comparisons corrections, particularly Bonferroni.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The ad alone made respondents value diversity of life experience in office less; presenting Jackson first increased its perceived value by 0.79 standard deviations relative to the ad alone. The narrative did not significantly change whether respondents thought diversity had to be paired with effective advocacy.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "It is possible that exposure to the Trump advertisement encouraged individuals to agree less in the value\nof diversity of life experiences",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "the initial exposure to the counter-narrative demonstrating a 0.79 SD unit increase in the perceived value of diversity\nof life experience as an important component of strengthening democracy, relative to those only presented with the Trump\nadvertisement before answering outcome questions",
            "page": 11
          },
          {
            "text": "there were\nno significant differences between the Counter-Narrative First and Trump Ad Alone groups on the outcome related to the\ncomplexity of representation",
            "page": 11
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Reading Jackson first reduced support for using the ad. The distinction between endorsement and effectiveness mattered: only the decline in explicit endorsement was significant when the two items were tested separately.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "exposure to the counter-narrative significantly decreased support for the Trump advertisement, relative to the Trump advertisement alone ( β̂2 = −0.172 SE = 0.048).",
            "page": 11
          },
          {
            "text": "Both coefficients are negative,\nincluding that of the item “This is an effective advertisement” and “I support the use of this advertisement”, though only the\nexplicit support for the advertisement item was statistically significant.",
            "page": 11
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "In secondary analyses, respondents affected by the justice system generally held more favorable views of people with records. Yet the ad’s negative effects on several individual judgments were larger for them, not smaller.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "those who identified as being system-impacted reported significantly more positive perceptions overall and within each\ntreatment arm, with only one exception.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "the Trump advertisement exposure appeared to be significantly more damaging\nto perceptions of individuals with criminal records for those who are system-impacted.",
            "page": 14
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "scope_and_limits": [
      {
        "claim": "The experiment cannot establish what Jackson’s narrative does on its own or whether the negative ad changes its effect. Budget constraints ruled out a narrative-only arm.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the ideal version of this survey experiment would have been to utilize a full factorial design (i.e., including\nthe exposure to the counter-narrative perspective arm alone). This was entirely done a result of binding budget constraints",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "the counter-narrative arm cannot be directly compared to the pure control arm to test the\nimpact of the counter-narrative alone because it also involves exposure to the Trump advertisement.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study identifies an effect of the ad as presented, not an independent effect of the word choice as distinct from the mugshot.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "as the advertisement (i.e., Trump’s mugshot with the words ‘convicted criminal’ imposed on the\nimage) was presented consistently across treatment arms, it is not possible to disentangle the impact of the image from\nthe stigmatizing terminology imposed",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The target population was the party’s own U.S. constituents who spoke fluent English, not voters across parties.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "This survey was fielded amongst individuals residing within\nthe United States who spoke fluent English and were members of the Democratic Party.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "thus the study sample focused only on their constituents.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Screening could not eliminate uncertainty about AI-generated online responses. The survey preceded Prolific’s own bot-detection rollout, and the prevalence of AI use for closed-ended surveys was unclear.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "this survey was completed before the introduction of Prolific’s bot detection",
            "page": 17
          },
          {
            "text": "the propensity for the standard online survey taker to utilize AI agents to\ncomplete close-ended surveys is not well understood.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Participants may have inferred the purpose and given socially desirable answers. The emotional-response check offered some evidence against that explanation without excluding it.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "it could be the case that participants were subject to the Hawthorne effect, resulting in\noutcomes being driven – in some capacity – by social desirability bias.",
            "page": 17
          },
          {
            "text": "helps alleviate some\nconcerns but does not rule this out.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "A single measurement after one exposure cannot establish how long either effect lasts or what repeated exposure would do.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "for how long do the effects of the counter-narrative\nprime before the advertisement and the advertisement itself persist; b) how may effects vary by single versus repeated\nexposure to the advertisement and/or the counter-narrative prime?",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The outcomes were reported attitudes, not decisions or actions. The experiment does not establish that either message changes voting behavior.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "to what extent would these treatments translate to shifted decision-making and behavior, as opposed to self-reported perceptions and attitudes?",
            "page": 17
          },
          {
            "text": "especially as it relates to potential\nvoting behavior, timing of future (field) experimental studies should be in line with election periods.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The survey was conducted after the 2024 election result was known. Its estimates are not a measurement of what actually happened during the campaign.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "this current study was conducted in July of 2025, after the outcome of the election was known and not actively\nduring the campaign. Whether these results replicate during campaign periods is an open question",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The measures did not exhaust the meanings of political representation, including policy agreement and symbolic versus active representation across identities.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "in terms of policy position, symbolic and active representation on myriad dimensions of identity, etc.), that may be additionally important to explore",
            "page": 18
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The five-item attitudes index had only marginally adequate reliability. In particular, the item asking whether criminal labels do more harm than good moved against expectations and may have measured something different.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "the Cronbach’s alpha coefficient was 0.71, reflecting the lower end of the threshold\nfor adequate reliability [59].",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "Both the Counter-Narrative First and the Control groups’ model-adjusted averages were significantly different from that\nof the Trump Ad Alone group for each individual item, though one was directionally opposite than what was hypothesized.",
            "page": 12
          },
          {
            "text": "as criminals may do more harm than good ( β̂0 = 0.328, SE = 0.048) and ( β̂2 = −0.441, SE = 0.047).",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "and/or that this is item measures something more distinct from perceptions of individuals\nwith records.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Racial attitudes were measured before treatment as covariates; the study did not test whether the ad activated racial stereotypes or changed those attitudes.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "racial attitudes were measured as covariates and presented before stimuli; however, understanding the potential for activation of negative stereotypes related to those with criminal records could very likely impact Democrats’ racial attitudes",
            "page": 18
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The results do not settle whether the narrative reduced stigma generally or encouraged respondents to distinguish people they consider deserving from others with records.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "While it is possible that the intervention constructs a new category of deservingness, rather than reducing stigma, the\nempirical relationship between attitudes and personal connection suggests that reduction of stigma is more likely [55–57].\nStill, future research should clarify this relationship more deeply.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "theoretical_positioning": [
      {
        "claim": "Harney extends social categorization theory to campaign messaging: repeatedly marking a salient political opponent as a member of a group can make judgments about him carry over to other members.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Leaning upon social categorization theory, propagating such\nlabels on Trump may thereby spillover, impacting constituents’ perspectives of individuals with records more broadly.",
            "page": 1
          },
          {
            "text": "theory and evidence would predict greater homogenization and spillover perceptions\nfrom Trump, such that perceptions of individuals with records would be worsened\namongst Democrats after exposure to the advertisement, including its affixed, “convicted criminal” label.",
            "page": 2
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The paper uses work on motivated reasoning and welfare stigma to distinguish policy support from regard for beneficiaries: Democrats’ relative opposition to punitive policies need not protect people with records from stigma.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "policy support is distinct from perceptions of individuals who would be directly\nimpacted by these policies. Perhaps the clearest example of this possibility in literature on the dynamics between policy\nsupport and perceptions of its beneficiaries is that of the social safety net.",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "Psychologically, individuals may be engaging in motivated reasoning, as rhetoric\naligning with prior beliefs about the beneficiaries (i.e., they must ‘deserve’ or ‘work for’ their benefits), is unchallenged",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Harney combines perspective-getting and counter-stereotype research with accounts of representation based on emotion and policy alignment. Jackson’s account was chosen both to invite empathy and to show what he advocates as a representative.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "this counter-narrative\nutilized perspective-getting, sharing the experience of a state Assemblymember who was previously incarcerated (i.e., has\na criminal record,) and is dedicated to using their platform to serve the public and advocate for system-impacted populations being represented in government.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "one that signals their policy alignment (i.e., dedication to justice system reform), would be well-suited to addressing both the affective and policy representation avenues for bolstering support.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "the strength of the counter-narrative is not\nunexpected, but promising, given prior evidence on the effects of perspective-getting and counter-stereotypical narratives",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Harney tentatively applies prospect theory to the representation finding: if the ad made Trump the reference point, other officials could look better without doing anything differently. This is offered as an interpretation, not an established mechanism.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Collectively, these results may\nsimply be Prospect Theory in action: Donald J. Trump may be serving as reference point that may influence Democrats to see other elected officials as comparatively better [73].",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "interlocutor_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Harney builds on Smith and Zarate and on Sherman: people with limited exposure to a group may judge unfamiliar members by a salient real member. The ad experiment asks whether Trump can serve as just such an exemplar for Democrats judging people with records.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "particularly salient members as exemplars",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "they\nmay be more likely to lean upon perceptions of group exemplars (i.e., real individuals\nwho are members of this group) to fill in the gaps for other members of that group,\nwith saliency of a group member increasing the propensity for that member to be\nan exemplar [20,21].",
            "page": 2
          },
          {
            "text": "In combination with the increased salience and potentially greater reliance on salient members as exemplars,\nsuch as Donald J. Trump, there could still be disconnect between policy support and public perceptions of those who are\nmost immediately impacted by these policies.",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
